namespace bo3z::codex
{
	struct Chapter { int book; std::string_view title, image; std::vector<std::string_view> paras; };
	inline constexpr std::array<std::string_view, 2> kBooks{ "The Curse of Alduin", "A Survivor's Guide" };
	inline const std::vector<Chapter> kChapters{
		{ 0, "The Greybeard's Account", "sigil", { "Set down at High Hrothgar by a Greybeard who has broken his silence, for whoever lived through Helgen" } },
		{ 0, "I. Three Words", "helgen", { "Helgen burned, and you lived. But Alduin did not flee: as he rose from the ash he shouted three words into the earth - SLEN TIID VO, Flesh, Time, Undo - the shout that raises the dead, and every barrow in Skyrim answered. His mist rolled over the door of Sovngarde, and now the dead of Skyrim cannot pass to Shor's hall: they lie restless in the ground, and when the sun goes down they claw their way up. The Dragon Priests who swore themselves to Alduin in the Merethic Era remember their oaths and wake with them. Only the Echo Altars the ancient Tongues raised in every hold can still his voice, and they have gone dark. Wake them, city by city, and their dead will sleep again.", "We heard it on the mountain. Not a shout to break stone, but Words sung slow, that sank into the earth and stayed there: <b>Slen</b>, flesh. <b>Tiid</b>, time. <b>Vo</b>, undo. It is the shout by which Alduin raises his fallen kin from their burial mounds, and this time he gave it to the earth itself. By day it sleeps in the ground. When the sun goes down it wakes, and every barrow, every crypt and every churchyard in the holds remembers it.", "Worse: his mist has rolled over the door of <b>Sovngarde</b>. The honoured dead cannot cross to Shor's hall, and a soul with nowhere to go stays with its body. That is why they rise. Free Sovngarde, and the Curse ends." } },
		{ 0, "II. The Dead Who Answer", "graves", { "From dusk until first light the dead climb out of the earth, more each night and harder to kill. At dawn they fall back to dust. The Nords of old had names for them, and the names are true again:", "<b>Haugbui</b>, the mound-dwellers - slow, many, never tired. <b>Pyre-Thralls</b>, the burned dead, still smouldering. <b>Gravegnawers</b>, quick and starved. <b>Barrow Archers</b> on the walls.", "The <b>Tongues</b> - Ash, Rime and Storm - were shouters once; their dead mouths still hold a word or two. <b>Fen-Wailers</b> scream, and the horde comes to the scream: kill them first. <b>Sewn Thralls</b> burst when they fall. <b>Grave-Hounds</b> run in packs, and the <b>Bone Jotunn</b> is the skeleton of a giant, walking.", "Over them all ride the great ones: the <b>Thanes of the Mound</b>, the <b>Mother of Mounds</b>, <b>Haugrisi the Mound-Giant</b>, <b>the Elk-Crowned</b>, <b>Grahzah</b>, Alduin's Grave-Wing, a dragon of bone, and on the Fog Nights <b>Grimmvar</b>, Alduin's Herald, a dragon of shadow - Vulmidviing in his own tongue, 'dark, loyal wings'. Look at a wounded thing and a pale bar shows how much life it has left; the great ones' bars burn gold and bear their names." } },
		{ 0, "III. The Echo Altars", "", { "This is not the first time Alduin has shouted the dead awake. In the Dragon War he did it once over the barrows of the Atmorans, and the Tongues - the first men to learn the Voice - answered him. They raised an altar in every hold where the dead marched: a star of violet fire caught in a ring of floating stones, bound with runes of blue, standing on a floor of black stone inlaid with gold.", "The fire is Alduin's own Thu'um, caught and turned back on itself. The runes are the Tongues' answer, holding it there. Every mage who fell in that war gave their last spell to the star, and the star kept it: that is why the altars have so many to give, and why the holds call them <b>Echo Altars</b>.", "When Alduin was cast out of time the altars went dark and sank into the earth, and towns grew over them. At Helgen he returned, and the altars woke - but only half. Each one has been silenced by one of the <b>Dragon Priests</b> who ruled Skyrim for Alduin in the Merethic Era: their oaths to him outlived them, and the draugr that guard their tombs were their servants in life. When Alduin returned, the oaths woke, and each priest came down from his tomb to the altar nearest it." } },
		{ 0, "IV. Waking an Altar", "wards", { "The altars answer to you. Perhaps it is because you heard the three Words at Helgen and lived; perhaps it is something older. Stand before one and it will set you <b>trials</b> - hold its runic circle while the dead come for you, cull the risen in its streets - and when they are done its Dragon Priest rises to stop you. Strike him down and the altar rings with the Tongues' answer. Its city becomes a sanctuary: no dead rise within its walls.", "There is no Word Wall at an altar. The Tongues cut their words into the altar's <b>heart-stone</b> instead - the great rock at its centre, ringed with runes. Once you have walked the Way of the Voice, stand at a woken altar and the runes give up their words to you, as a Word Wall would: the city's own <b>Thu'um</b>." } },
		{ 0, "V. The Way of the Voice", "", { "Arngeir will tell you, when you have climbed the Seven Thousand Steps, that the altars are older than High Hrothgar. Jurgen Windcaller's Tongues found them after Red Mountain, already ancient, and kept them as he taught: the Voice for worship, not for war. They wrote down what each altar holds - the pages a woken altar gives you, <i>The Broken Words</i>, are theirs.", "Those who stand against the dead with you have their own reasons. The conjurers and battle-mages of the <b>College of Winterhold</b> have seen Morokei walk again; the <b>Dawnguard</b> hunt what rises at night; the <b>Vigilants of Stendarr</b> will not let the dead walk while they draw breath." } },
		{ 0, "VI. The Voices of the Holds", "", { "<b>Riverwood</b>: <i>Thunder Lash</i> (STRUN BAH QO, 'Storm Wrath Lightning'). Lightning strikes the nearest dead - three, five or eight bolts, each leaping to one more.", "<b>Solitude</b>: <i>Raise the Fallen</i> (DIL VAAZ ZOL, 'Undead Tear Zombie'). The fallen rise to fight for you - two, four or six of them - and burst when their time is up.", "<b>Windhelm</b>: <i>Frost Nova</i> (IIZ SLEN NUS, 'Ice Flesh Statue'). A ring of frost freezes everything around you solid, and the frozen shatter when struck.", "<b>Markarth</b>: <i>Dwemer Quake</i> (GOL VAH DEN, 'Earth Spring Hold'). The ground heaves in front of you and knocks the dead down - with the third word, stone spikes erupt through them.", "<b>Riften</b>: <i>Shadow Step</i> (FEIM ZII GRON, 'Fade Spirit Bind'). You blink forward through the horde, leaving a shadow that draws the dead to it and then bursts.", "<b>Falkreath</b>: <i>Death's Harvest</i> (KRII LUN AUS, 'Kill Leech Suffer'). A draining cone that heals you for every one of the dead it touches - the third word also raises a fallen friend nearby.", "<b>Morthal</b>: <i>Mire Grasp</i> (ZUN HAAL VIIK, 'Weapon Hand Defeat'). Roots burst from the ground where you look, holding the dead in place and poisoning them.", "<b>Dawnstar</b>: <i>Nightmare Howl</i> (FAAS RU MAAR, 'Fear Run Terror'). The dead are thrown back in terror and slowed - and the weakest die of fright.", "<b>Winterhold</b>: <i>Arcane Singularity</i> (SU GRAH DUN, 'Air Battle Grace'). A vortex pulls the dead together where you look, then detonates.", "<b>Whiterun</b>: <i>Dragon's Updraft</i>. A whirlwind lifts you high - and with it, a dragon that will carry you." } },
		{ 0, "Epilogue", "", { "Every Echo Altar in Skyrim is awake. Alduin's voice lingers only in the wild places, and the mist will not lift from Sovngarde's door until the World-Eater falls.", "<i>- written in the silence of High Hrothgar</i>" } },
		{ 1, "Before Dusk", "", { "Notes left at the Sleeping Giant Inn by one who made it this far. Read them before dusk." } },
		{ 1, "Day and Night", "", { "A day lasts about fifteen minutes. By day the dead sleep: rest, explore, spend what you earned. At dusk they rise in <b>waves</b>, clawing up out of the ground at the same places every night. Each wave brings twenty more than the last: twenty, forty, sixty... The first night has five waves; every night adds one more (never more than 2,000 dead a night for each player). The day lasts about 18 minutes. The night does not end until the last wave is down - then dawn breaks.", "You cannot sleep or wait while the dead walk." } },
		{ 1, "Septims", "", { "Every risen thing you kill leaves septims. A headshot leaves more, and a kill with a blade or a spell more still. Septims are the only coin the Echo Altars take.", "Watch the dead for tokens: a skull (Insta-Kill), a coin (Double Points), a falling star (it destroys every corpse risen tonight) and a chest (a Fire Sale at the altar)." } },
		{ 1, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Look into its star and press Activate. Its menu does not stop the night. Move through it with the D-pad (or W and S) and take a row with A (or Enter); B leaves.", "<b>Call a spell</b> - the altar turns and gives you one of the spells of the fallen. Each call costs more than the last until dawn. You carry two of its spells; a third asks which one it replaces.", "<b>Call a bow</b> - the altar's second wheel. Six elemental bows: fire, frost, storm, wind, sun and blood. You carry one; a new one takes its place.", "<b>Reforge</b> - make the spell or bow in your hands stronger, three times over. Every tier gives a bow a new attack: the fire bow's arrows burst, then leave burning ground, then a full draw calls down a volley of meteors.", "<b>Perks</b> - lasting gifts. You carry five at most.", "<b>Trials</b> - the quest that wakes the altar. It offers five; take up any three, in any order (step over to them with D-pad left or right, or A and D). When three are done the city's Dragon Priest rises at night near the altar. Slay it and the city is safe for good. Skyrim's difficulty sets how hard the trials are." } },
		{ 1, "The Five Trials", "", { "<b>Ward of the Tongues</b> - stand in the runic circle round the altar while the dead come faster. Step out and the count waits for you.", "<b>The Culling</b> - kill a number of the risen in the city.", "<b>The Unbroken</b> - clear whole waves without falling below half health. Fall below and that wave does not count.", "<b>Trial of Steel</b> - only kills with a melee weapon or your fists count.", "<b>The Long Night's Breath</b> - stay by the altar while the night's waves are cleared." } },
		{ 1, "Spells", "", { "Tap the attack to throw a bolt. Hold it, and both hands gather the spell's full power: the longer you hold, the harder it strikes. Let go too soon and the power gutters out. Magicka comes back fast." } },
		{ 1, "Shouts", "", { "Once you have walked the Way of the Voice, the runes on a woken altar's heart-stone teach you its city's Thu'um - no Word Wall needed. Equip it like any shout. Its cooldown shows under your spells, bottom right." } },
		{ 1, "The Dragon", "", { "Wake Whiterun's altar and a dragon will answer you. Jump and press Activate in mid-air: it swoops down and you take hold of its harness.", "Steer with the stick or WASD. Forward dives, back climbs. Attack to make it breathe fire on the dead below - only the dead, never your friends. At the altar you can make deeper bonds with it." } },
		{ 1, "Echo Stones and the Reliquary", "", { "When a city's altar is freed and its night won, its heart-stone turns to blue light. Step onto the altar and it gives you a page: <i>The Broken Words</i>, one for each city - a Tongue's writing on that city's Dragon Priest and on the words cut into its heart-stone. Once you have walked the Way of the Voice, the heart-stone's runes teach you the city's Thu'um.", "Some parts fall from the Dragon Priests. Some wait in reliquaries at old barrows, guarded, and only wake at night. Some must be earned by killing the dead near a barrow's door.", "Take them to <b>High Hrothgar</b>: a sealed door in the great hall leads down to the Reliquary, where each wonder is forged on its pedestal once its three parts are found." } },
		{ 1, "The Wonder Weapons", "", { "A forged wonder is taken up from its pedestal and cast from your hands like the altar's spells: tap to strike, hold both hands to unleash it. One at a time - return it to take another.", "<b>Gravecaller</b> - grave-wisps hunt the dead and feed you their life. Unleashed: three bone warriors claw out of the earth to fight for you.", "<b>Frostbinder Gauntlets</b> - ice binds what it strikes; strike a bound foe and it shatters, cutting those round it. Unleashed: everything near you freezes, then bursts.", "<b>Wolf Queen's Crown</b> - spirit-wolves run down the dead in pairs. Unleashed: the Queen howls and her spectral pack runs at your side.", "<b>Horn of the Dragon Priests</b> - a war-cry hurls the dead back. Unleashed: the old war-host charges down the line in front of you." } },
		{ 1, "The Three Words", "", { "With your class's wonder in hand, Alduin's three words wake one after another: <b>SLEN</b> at the cult's old shrine in the Reach, <b>TIID</b> in the courtyard of Labyrinthian, <b>VO</b> on the summit of the Throat of the World. Your compass marks the word that is awake.", "Go there at night. Its dead rise round you in waves; when the last wave falls, the word's bearer rises. Kill it and the word is silenced for good, and its power stays with you: <b>SLEN</b> - everything you wield hits 15% harder; <b>TIID</b> - the night's blows hurt you 20% less; <b>VO</b> - your magicka returns half again as fast.", "Leave the place, or let dawn come, and the word falls quiet until you return. Silence all three and the curse of Alduin is broken." } },
		{ 1, "The Hall of the Awakened", "", { "With the curse broken, the Reliquary under High Hrothgar becomes the <b>Hall of the Awakened</b>. Its stations:", "<b>Alduin's Bane</b> - the story's last weapon, a greatsword forged from the World-Eater's defeat: dragonfire on every blow, a crescent of golden fire on a power attack, and when its soul meter fills, Alduin's shade rains meteors round you. While you hold it, kills heal you and your shouts return twice as fast. <b>Robes of the First Dragonborn</b> - wear them and spells and wonders cost a quarter less magicka, and it returns faster. <b>The Bone-Wyrm's Skull</b> - your glider's dragon wears Alduin's bones (touch it again to change back).", "<b>Brazier of the Endless Night</b> - while it burns, the night never ends: wave after wave, until you put it out. <b>Seat of the Awakened</b> - begin again: every city falls back under the curse and the nights start over, the dead half again as tough each cycle. Your wonders, bows, powers and the words' gifts stay yours." } },
		{ 1, "Whispers of the Altars", "", { "Every freed altar keeps a secret. Nobody will tell you what it is. Stand at a freed altar and its <b>whispers</b> show in its menu - another line for each step you find. These are the first:", "<b>Riverwood</b> - The Giant sleeps, but his cups are scattered about the village after dark.", "<b>Whiterun</b> - The Gildergreen weeps golden sap - but only flame will loosen it.", "<b>Solitude</b> - Her braziers remember the order she lit them in.", "<b>Windhelm</b> - Five horns lie frozen in the ice around the city.", "<b>Markarth</b> - Four resonators hum beneath the stone city's shadow.", "<b>Riften</b> - Three shadow coins, taken by night.", "<b>Falkreath</b> - Six graves cry out to be fed.", "<b>Morthal</b> - Lanterns burn in the marsh - but not for long.", "<b>Dawnstar</b> - Shards of a nightmare litter the cold shore.", "<b>Winterhold</b> - Four anomalies drift at the cliff's edge." } },
		{ 1, "Event Nights and Themed Waves", "", { "From night 8, about one night in eight is an <b>event night</b>: a Goblin Raid, a Harpy Swarm, Armored Champions or a Blood Fog. The whole night belongs to it - and it pays <b>double septims</b>, with the dead coming twice as fast.", "On ordinary nights, from the third wave, about one wave in six is <b>themed</b> the same way. Its name shows on the wave dial under the compass.", "About one night in five, halfway through, <b>something stirs in the dark</b>: a mini-boss. It always drops a wonder weapon part - the next one you're missing - for everyone in the party." } },
		{ 1, "Falling", "", { "At every dawn, and whenever an altar wakes, the day is saved. If you fall, you wake at that dawn. In a party, stand over a fallen friend and hold Activate to lift them.", "Good luck. Stay out of the barrows after dark." } },
	};
}
